1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:10,818
The configurable logic block is where a design's logic actually runs. bob's follows AMD's 7-series slice for the element, and OpenFPGA's clustered architecture for the block.

2
00:00:12,400 --> 00:00:15,654
A bob logic block is a cluster of 4 logic elements.

3
00:00:15,974 --> 00:00:20,747
16 signals come in from the routing, and 8 leave, two from each element.

4
00:00:21,200 --> 00:00:30,049
Between the inputs and the elements sits a full crossbar: any element input can take any of the sixteen block inputs, or any element's output fed back.

5
00:00:30,369 --> 00:00:34,308
Neighbouring logic talks through it without using the general routing at all.

6
00:00:34,767 --> 00:00:42,456
A carry chain runs up through the four elements, and on into the block above, over a dedicated wire with no configuration bits at all.

7
00:00:42,933 --> 00:00:43,864
Why four?

8
00:00:44,184 --> 00:00:46,150
It was measured, not guessed.

9
00:00:46,470 --> 00:00:52,649
A sweep built clusters of four, six, eight and ten elements, with full and half crossbars.

10
00:00:52,969 --> 00:01:01,493
Bigger clusters cost more host logic per element, because every crossbar mux is host logic, and half crossbars needed far wider channels.

11
00:01:01,813 --> 00:01:03,750
Four with a full crossbar won.

12
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:08,058
Each element input has its own crossbar mux.

13
00:01:08,378 --> 00:01:10,204
Its select is five bits.

14
00:01:10,524 --> 00:01:21,208
Value zero means constant zero, one means constant one, and two onward pick the 24 sources in order: the sixteen block inputs, then the eight element outputs.

15
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:28,493
So the feature e0 dot x0 equal to two connects block input zero to element zero's first LUT input.

16
00:01:28,813 --> 00:01:33,120
Six inputs on four elements is twenty-four such muxes in every block.

17
00:01:34,700 --> 00:01:42,215
Inside one element, the six-input LUT is stored as two halves of thirty-two bits, each addressed by inputs zero to four.

18
00:01:42,535 --> 00:01:48,912
Input five chooses between the halves, through a MUXF7, exactly as in AMD's LUT6.

19
00:01:49,367 --> 00:01:52,816
Set the flag frac, and the select is forced high.

20
00:01:53,136 --> 00:02:02,055
Now the element is two five-input LUTs sharing their inputs: O6 is the upper half, and O5, the lower half, comes out separately.

21
00:02:02,375 --> 00:02:07,458
This is the fracturable LUT, UG474's LUT6 underscore 2.

22
00:02:07,933 --> 00:02:16,104
O6 feeds the carry logic and a small mux that picks what the first flip-flop stores: the carry sum, O6, or O5.

23
00:02:16,424 --> 00:02:18,744
O5 feeds the second flip-flop.

24
00:02:19,064 --> 00:02:25,569
And a last mux on each side, set by a configuration bit, picks whether the output is registered or not.

25
00:02:27,133 --> 00:02:34,022
For addition, the LUT computes the propagate signal: for bit k of a plus b, that is a exclusive-or b.

26
00:02:34,342 --> 00:02:41,909
The carry multiplexer then either passes the incoming carry along, when propagate is one, or generates a new carry from a.

27
00:02:42,229 --> 00:02:44,218
An exclusive-or makes the sum.

28
00:02:44,700 --> 00:02:46,860
Seven plus one shows the point.

29
00:02:47,180 --> 00:02:53,401
The carry ripples up through all four elements with no trip through the general routing, and on into the logic block above.

30
00:02:53,721 --> 00:02:57,120
That is why synthesis maps every adder onto this chain.

31
00:02:58,700 --> 00:03:04,542
Both flip-flops follow AMD's FDRE and FDSE rules, in strict priority.

32
00:03:04,862 --> 00:03:12,214
Global set-reset comes first and loads the flip-flop's INIT value; it is held during configuration and released at startup.

33
00:03:12,534 --> 00:03:17,993
Nothing else can change the flip-flop unless writes are enabled and the user clock enable is high.

34
00:03:18,467 --> 00:03:24,783
Then the routed synchronous reset loads the reset value, and only then does the clock enable let D through.

35
00:03:25,103 --> 00:03:31,929
The CE and SR pins are shared by all eight flip-flops of a block, as one slice shares them on AMD's parts.

36
00:03:32,400 --> 00:03:36,582
Milestone 26 split the initial value from the reset value.

37
00:03:36,902 --> 00:03:43,419
A register declared as one, with a reset to zero, now starts at one, as it would on real silicon.

38
00:03:43,739 --> 00:03:45,979
The board check init-srval proves it.

39
00:03:47,533 --> 00:03:51,275
An adder normally spends a whole LUT computing a exclusive-or b.

40
00:03:51,595 --> 00:03:59,016
Double Duty, from a 2025 paper, feeds the adder's two operands straight from two element inputs instead.

41
00:03:59,467 --> 00:04:07,071
The LUT is then free, and its lower half drives the element's second output as an independent four-input function, beside the adder.

42
00:04:07,391 --> 00:04:10,134
One flag, dd, turns it on.

43
00:04:10,600 --> 00:04:23,107
VPR's packer cannot aim for it, so it saved under five percent. bob's own packer fills the free LUTs of adder elements on purpose, and used thirteen point two percent fewer elements over the example designs.

44
00:04:24,667 --> 00:04:34,875
Every element is configured by exactly 109 bits: six crossbar selects of five bits, 15 flags, and sixty-four bits of truth table.

45
00:04:35,333 --> 00:04:53,025
The flags are the choices you have just seen: fracture, carry on, the generate source, which value each flip-flop stores, whether each output is registered, whether each flip-flop uses the clock enable and the reset, the two reset values and two initial values, and Double Duty.

46
00:04:53,500 --> 00:04:58,211
Four elements and three bits of padding make 439 bits per block.

47
00:04:58,531 --> 00:05:02,883
The routing multiplexers of that tile follow them in the configuration memory.

48
00:05:04,433 --> 00:05:09,971
Put together, an AND of two inputs in element zero of block x2 y3 is three settings.

49
00:05:10,291 --> 00:05:15,200
Two crossbar selects bring block inputs zero and one to LUT inputs zero and one.

50
00:05:15,520 --> 00:05:17,573
The truth table is sixteen eights.

51
00:05:17,893 --> 00:05:22,923
Everything else stays zero, which means constant inputs and an unregistered output.

52
00:05:24,500 --> 00:05:27,627
Finally, how this is built inside the host chip.

53
00:05:27,947 --> 00:05:34,951
A bob truth table is configuration, so it could sit in sixty-four flip-flops behind a sixty-four-way mux.

54
00:05:35,271 --> 00:05:36,664
That is far too big.

55
00:05:36,984 --> 00:05:42,723
Instead it lives in AMD's CFGLUT5: a real LUT whose table is shifted in serially.

56
00:05:43,200 --> 00:05:48,956
Each crossbar mux is five such LUTs, each looking at five sources, and a fixed OR.

57
00:05:49,276 --> 00:05:58,192
The selected source's leaf holds a table that copies that one input; every other leaf holds zeros, so the OR passes exactly the chosen signal.

58
00:05:58,667 --> 00:06:05,712
That is 128 CFGLUT5s per block, loaded automatically as their configuration frame arrives.

59
00:06:06,032 --> 00:06:13,384
It cut a block from roughly four hundred and seventy host LUTs to under two hundred, and it is what let the grid reach ten by ten.

60
00:06:14,967 --> 00:06:28,978
The element is ble dot s v; the crossbar and the cluster are generated into the fabric; and the cluster testbench checks every flag, every LUT mode and every crossbar value against the Python model, at LUT size six and again at four.

